      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter this order, and your confirmation doesn't change that. You said you're not a clinician, so you can't confirm that this dose is clinically appropriate. The problem is the dose itself. 6,400 mg/day is twice the maximum daily ibuprofen dose, and the risk of serious harm is real, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nHere is what can move this forward:\n\n- **A prescriber enters or verifies the order.** If a physician really intends this dose, they should enter it directly and document the reason. Pharmacy will probably review it as well.\n- **The dose is corrected.** If the intended order was lower, I can enter it once a prescriber confirms the exact dose. Examples are 400\u2013800 mg every 6\u20138 hours, or 800 mg three times daily (2,400 mg/day).\n\nIf you give me a prescriber-confirmed dose that falls within standard limits, I'll enter it right away."
      }
     ],
